ieve they have been terminated unfairly for medical reasons, 40 \nappeal of faculty who believe they have been victims of discrimination in nonreappointment,  \n 40-41 \nappeal of faculty who have not been terminated but who believe that their academic freedom  \n has been abridged, 41 \nappeal of post-tenure review decisions, 27-30 \nappeal of promotion decisions, 27 \nappeal of tenure decisions, 30  \nFaculty Grievance Committee, 26-27, 29-30, 33-43, 99, 102, 105 \nMediation Committee, 35 \nSee Policy and Procedures concerning Academic Freedom and Tenure, Procedures for \nFaculty Grievance Cases Not Covered by the Policy on Academic Freedom and Tenure. \nHolidays, 70 \nHonor Council, 48 \nHuman Resource records, 82 \nInsurance benefits, 70-71 \nIntellectual Property Policy, 6-8 \nKeys, loss and replacement of, 82 \nLeave policies, 56-70 \nannual leave, 5, 66-68 \nclass, absence from, 45 \ncourt leave, 62 \n135 \n \ndeath in immediate family, 62 \nFamily and Medical Leave Act (FMLA), 59-61, 85 \nholidays, 70 \nleave of absence without pay, 55, 68-69 \nLeave Transfer Program, 69 \nmilitary leave, 63 \nreassigned time, 50, 63 \nsabbatical leave, 2, 28, 63-66 \nsick leave 57-59 \nWorkers' Compensation, 61 \nLibrary, Bylaws of, 125-126 \n   amendments, 126 \n   committees, 125 \n   election of faculty senators, 90-92 \n   meetings, 125 \n   membership, 125-126 \n   voting rights, 125 \nLibrary faculty, 18-21 \nevaluation of, 18-21 \nmembership of the faculty, 128 \nvoting rights of, 128 \nLost and found, 82 \nMerit raises, 11 \nannual performance rating, 15 \nevaluation data for, 11-15 \nSee Policy on Teaching Responsibility. \nMoral turpitude, definition of, 33 \nNepotism policy, 11 \nNonrenewal of Appointment, Notice of, 15 \nOccupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) guidelines, 86 \nParking regulations, 83 \nPayroll deductions, 72-73 \nPeer review, 13, 15, 17, 27-30 \nSee Policy and Procedures for Post-Tenure Review of Faculty, third-year peer review. \nPolitical activity of faculty, 55 \nPost-Tenure Review of Faculty, Policy and Procedures for, 27-30 \nSee grievance procedures. \nPresident \nduties of, 1, 2, 4, 8-11, 16, 22-24, 27-28, 30, 33, 36-37, 41, 44, 49, 50-52, 54-55, 59, 69-70, \n74-75, 88, 90-92, 95-97, 102-103, 109-110, 113-114, 116, 119, 124-126, 128-131, 133-138 \nsee Bylaws of the Board of Trustees. \nevaluation of, 1, 49-51  \nselection of, communication of faculty views to the Board of Trustees, 1 \nterm of office of, 1 \nPresident's Senior Staff, 1 \nPrivacy issues concerning e-mail, 81 \n136 \n \nProfessor Emeritus/Emerita status, 9 \nPromotion \nclassroom observation for, 14-15 \nCriteria and Procedures for Promotion in Academic Rank, 24-27 \nduring sabbatical leave, 63-66 \nevaluation data for, 22, 25-27 \ntenure and promotion committees of the College, the Schools, and the Library role in \npromotion process of, 107, 108-109, 111, 115, 122, 125, 128 \nSee grievance procedures, Policy on Teaching Responsibility. \nProvost \nduties of, 1-2, 4-10, 14-17, 22-31, 36-37, 45-49, 52, 55-58, 63-66, 68-69, 74, 90-93, 95-96, \n102-103, 107-111, 113-114, 116, 119, 124-126, 128-129 \nevaluation of, 51-53 \nterm of office of, 1 \nReappointment, notice of, 15 \nReassigned time, 50, 63, 109 \nProfessional Development Committee, 50, 63, 96, 98, 108-109 \nRequired courses, 8-9 \nRetirement plans, 75-80 \nService credit, purchase of, 67, 76-80 \nRogers Library, faculty and staff use of, 82 \nSabbaticals, 28, 49, 63-65, 107 \nProfessional Development Committee, 49-50, 63-66, 96, 98, 108-109 \nScholarly activity, policies concerning \ncopyright regulations, 6-7, 48 \ngrant application, 50 \nintegrity in research and scholarship, 49 \nprofessional development, 49-50 \nreassigned time, 63 \nsabbatical leave, 63-66 \nsponsored research, 5 \nSchool of Business, Bylaws of, 113-117 \namendments, 117 \ncommittees, 115-116 \nelection of faculty senators, 90-92 \nmeetings, 116 \nmembership, 113 \nvoting rights, 113 \nSchool 